# Summary
Introduced: active PG records do not bind an epoch to a specific peered primary, and epoch changes can leave PGs Active while `serving_pg_primary` selects whichever acting-set node first observes the new epoch.
The commit introduces durable PG acting-set/state records and a new `serving_pg_primary` API. `complete_pg_peering` verifies that the supplied primary is currently deterministic, but then only stores `PgState::Active` and bumps the cluster epoch; it does not store that primary in the PG record. After that epoch bump, all nodes have stale `last_observed_epoch` values until they heartbeat again. `serving_pg_primary` then recomputes the primary dynamically as the first acting-set member that is healthy and has observed the current epoch. This means an acting-set member that was not the peering-completing primary can heartbeat first and become the serving primary while the PG remains Active, without a peering transition. The same issue can happen after unrelated membership, endpoint, or incarnation changes that bump the cluster epoch without moving active PGs to Peering. In a multihost deployment using this control plane for routing writes, this can cause primary changes without command-log/shard peering, risking stale-primary behavior, split-brain writes, or data loss. The code is not currently wired into the public S3 HTTP path, but the introduced control-plane API itself has an unsafe primary-selection invariant.

