## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust integration-test PoC using the public storage::control_plane API. Code review evidence: PgControlRecord stores only pg_id/state/acting_set and no peered primary at crates/storage/src/control_plane.rs:247-252; complete_pg_peering validates the supplied primary via deterministic_pg_primary at lines 610-614, but persists only PgState::Active and bumps the epoch at lines 622-625; NodeControlRecord::can_serve_primary requires last_observed_epoch == cluster_epoch at lines 182-185; serving_pg_primary dynamically recomputes from current heartbeat state at lines 761-776. The PoC creates nodes 1 and 2, catches both up, sets PG 42 acting_set=[1,2], completes peering with NodeId(1), then sends the first post-peering-epoch heartbeat from NodeId(2). The PG remains Active, but serving_pg_primary(pg) returns Some(NodeId(2)). Reproduction command: cargo test -p storage --test control_plane_primary_poc active_pg_primary_can_change_after_peering_epoch_bump -- --nocapture. Key output: PG state=Active, primary_that_completed_peering=NodeId(1), observed_primary_after_node2_heartbeat=Some(NodeId(2)), epoch=7; assertion failed at crates/storage/tests/control_plane_primary_poc.rs:97 because left was Some(NodeId(2)) and right was Some(NodeId(1)). This demonstrates an Active PG primary change without another peering transition. I also attempted memory-style validation per instructions: valgrind and gdb were not installed; ASan failed because this rustc rejects -C sanitizer=address; LLDB was available and captured a non-interactive backtrace stopping at the PoC assertion panic.

